# Line endings.
#
# `text=auto` normalises to LF in the repository and lets git check out the
# platform's native ending in a working tree — which is what you want for
# source, and exactly wrong for a byte-for-byte fixture.
#
# `packages/notebook` asserts that its serializer reproduces `nbformat.writes`
# byte for byte against `fixtures/sample.ipynb`. On a Windows runner git checked
# that fixture out with CRLF, so the test compared LF output against a CRLF
# expectation and failed on every line of a 200-line notebook — a green macOS
# and Linux build and a red Windows one, for a difference the product does not
# have. Any file whose exact bytes are the assertion must be pinned to LF.
* text=auto

*.ipynb text eol=lf
*.svg    text eol=lf
*.bib    text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Fixtures are compared byte for byte. Never let a checkout rewrite them.
**/fixtures/** text eol=lf
**/__fixtures__/** text eol=lf
examples/** text eol=lf

# Binary, so git never tries.
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.icns binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
